= Long-term outcomes of cruciate ligament injury: a revised matched cohort analysis of New Zealand linked register data. Analysis plan
#smallcaps[Published]\ 06 June 2023

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Pryymachenko Y, Wilson R, Abbott JH, Bell R. Long-term outcomes of cruciate ligament injury: a revised matched cohort analysis of New Zealand linked register data. Analysis plan. OSF, 06 June 2023. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/9yu5r\")[10.17605/osf.io/9yu5r]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
Cruciate ligament (CL) injuries  are serious and common  conditions.  However,  very
little is known  on long-term  health and economic  effects of these injuries.  Our
previous  study has investigated these effects for  New Zealand (NZ)  population  using  a
rigorous  matching technique  (matching  individuals with a CL injury to similar
individuals  without  a CL injury).  However, despite  controlling  for  a large
set of baseline  characteristics aiming to capture individuals’ demographic, health,
and socio-economic status to the extent that the effects on long-term  outcomes  would  only
come from  the differences  in CL injury status, we came to the conclusion  that
our  previous  results were likely driven  by unobserved differences  in physical
activity levels, as people  who have a CL injury are likely to be more  active
than people  who do not (e.g.  65% of ACL surgeries  in NZ are sports-related).  Therefore,  the
current analysis aims at removing  these unobserved  differences  by using  a
control group  with a similar activity level as the treatment group  to identify  the
long-term  effects of CL injury.  This analysis plan details the methods  to be
used in this research.

